Here is the commit message:
bugfix

Quantifiers are no Application terms any more.

Here is the root cause:
The `isAtom` method's switch statement in `Z3UnsafeFormulaManager` did not have an explicit case for `Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST`, so quantifier terms fell through to the `default` branch and were incorrectly treated as atomic/application terms. As a result, quantifier formulas (e.g., created by `forall`) were misclassified, and subsequent introspection calls like `getArity` and `getArg` (which internally call Z3's `get_app_num_args`/`get_app_arg`) could be invoked on quantifier AST nodes instead of genuine `Z3_APP_AST` nodes. Since quantifiers are not application terms in Z3, this caused incorrect behavior/errors during formula introspection (e.g., in `isQuantifier`/`isForall` checks), which is why the test previously had to skip Z3 specifically.

Here is the patch:

   @Override
   public boolean isAtom(Long t) {
     int astKind = get_ast_kind(z3context, t);
     switch (astKind) {
       case Z3_APP_AST:
         long decl = get_app_decl(z3context, t);
         return !NON_ATOMIC_OP_TYPES.contains(get_decl_kind(z3context, decl));
+      case Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST:
+        return false;
       default:
         return true;
     }
   }

   @Override
   public int getArity(Long t) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_num_args(z3context, t);
   }

   @Override
   public Long getArg(Long t, int n) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_arg(z3context, t, n);
   }



